p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =3A-(-7)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =3A+7I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-140521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "15cb6b92d182e5278ecaaaa3821a9a850fd84daaabbb087c401a2c99368d7107"} {"question" "q-140521" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-140521" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-8A+7I"] ["math" "3A+4I"] ["math" "3A+7I"] ["math" "3A-2I"] ["math" "A+7I"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-140521" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3A+7I"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-83396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\text{tr}(A)=-7$ and $\\text{det}(A)=17.$ Then, ${A}^{2}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-83396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =-7A-17I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-83396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "15cb6b92d182e5278ecaaaa3821a9a850fd84daaabbb087c401a2c99368d7107"} {"question" "q-83396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-83396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-17A-7I"] ["math" "-7A+17I"] ["math" "-7A-17I"] ["math" "17A+7I"] ["math" "7A-17I"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-83396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7A-17I"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-83398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\text{tr}(A)=-14$ and $\\text{det}(A)=-36.$ Then, ${A}^{2}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-83398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =-14A+36I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-83398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "15cb6b92d182e5278ecaaaa3821a9a850fd84daaabbb087c401a2c99368d7107"} {"question" "q-83398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-83398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-14A+36I"] ["math" "-14A-36I"] ["math" "-36A+14I"] ["math" "14A-36I"] ["math" "36A-14I"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-83398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-14A+36I"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-37831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-37831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+1) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}-4A-5I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}-4A-5I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}+2A-4I-5{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-37831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "15cb6b92d182e5278ecaaaa3821a9a850fd84daaabbb087c401a2c99368d7107"} {"question" "q-37831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-37831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2I+4A+{A}^{2}"] ["math" "-5I-4A-5{A}^{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{4}{5}{A}^{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{1}{5}A+\\frac{2}{5}{A}^{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-37831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-38704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value $p+q.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-38704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-8A+6I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-8A+6I) & =O \\\\ A-8I+6{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 6{A}^{-1} & =8I-A \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{4}{3}I-\\frac{1}{6}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{4}{3},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{6}$ and\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{4}{3}+(-\\frac{1}{6})=\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-38704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "15cb6b92d182e5278ecaaaa3821a9a850fd84daaabbb087c401a2c99368d7107"} {"question" "q-38704" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-38704" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "-\\frac{7}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-38704" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-37779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-37779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-8A+9I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-8A+9I) & =O \\\\ A-8I+9{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{8}{9}I-\\frac{1}{9}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{8}{9},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{9},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{8}{9}+(-\\frac{1}{9})=\\frac{7}{9}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-37779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "15cb6b92d182e5278ecaaaa3821a9a850fd84daaabbb087c401a2c99368d7107"} {"question" "q-37779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-37779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{7}"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-37779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{9}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-40173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],$ and ${A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-40173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-5A-2 & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-5A-2) & ={A}^{-1}O \\\\ A-5I-2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =-\\frac{5}{2}I+\\frac{1}{2}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{5}{2},$ $q=\\frac{1}{2}$ and\n\n$$\np+q=-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-40173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "15cb6b92d182e5278ecaaaa3821a9a850fd84daaabbb087c401a2c99368d7107"} {"question" "q-40173" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-40173" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-40173" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-85929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-85929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((5-\\lambda )^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +29) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}+12{\\lambda }^{2}-49\\lambda +58 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{A}^{3}+12{A}^{2}-49A+58I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}(-{A}^{3}+12{A}^{2}-49A+58I) & =O \\\\ -{A}^{2}+12A-49I+58{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{58}(49I-12A+{A}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-85929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "15cb6b92d182e5278ecaaaa3821a9a850fd84daaabbb087c401a2c99368d7107"} {"question" "q-85929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-85929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "49I-12A+{A}^{2}"] ["math" "9I+2A+{A}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{58}(49I-12A+{A}^{2})"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}(49I-12A+{A}^{2})"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}(49I-12A+{A}^{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-85929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{58}(49I-12A+{A}^{2})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-85931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}-x-20,$ then ${A}^{3}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-85931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}-x-20,$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,x$ | $+$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-x-20\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{3}\\,\\,}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | $-$ | ${x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $20x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $20x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | ${x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $x$ | $-$ | $20$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $21x$ | $+$ | $20$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{3}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =q(x)p(x)+(21x+20),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)=x+1.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{3}=q(A)p(A)+(21A+20I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & =q(A)p(A)+(21A+20I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(21A+20I) \\\\  & =21A+20I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-85931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "15cb6b92d182e5278ecaaaa3821a9a850fd84daaabbb087c401a2c99368d7107"} {"question" "q-85931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-85931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-20A-21I"] ["math" "-21A+20I"] ["math" "20A+21I"] ["math" "21A+20I"] ["math" "21A-20I"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-85931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "21A+20I"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-38727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{aligned}]$\nand $f(x)={x}^{23}-25{x}^{21}-4x+2$, find $f(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-38727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 10 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(5-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda ) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{21}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-25\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{23}\\,-\\,25{x}^{21}\\,-\\,4x\\,+\\,2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{23}$ | $-$ | $25{x}^{21}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  | $-$ | $4x$ | $+$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(-4x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{21}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(-4A+2I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(-4A+2I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(-4A+2I) \\\\  & =-4A+2I \\\\  & =-4[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-20 & 0 \\\\ -40 & 20\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 0 \\\\ -40 & 22\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-38727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "15cb6b92d182e5278ecaaaa3821a9a850fd84daaabbb087c401a2c99368d7107"} {"question" "q-38727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-38727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 40 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 0 \\\\ -40 & 22\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-20 & 22 \\\\ -42 & 20\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-20 & 40 \\\\ 0 & 20\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ 40 & -22\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-38727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 0 \\\\ -40 & 22\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-37777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+4x+3,$ then ${A}^{4}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-37777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{4}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+4x+3,$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $4x$ | $+$ | $13$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+4x+3\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{4}\\,\\,\\,}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | $+$ | $4{x}^{3}$ | $+$ | $3{x}^{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | $4{x}^{3}$ | $-$ | $3{x}^{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | $4{x}^{3}$ | $-$ | $16{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $12x$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $13{x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $12x$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $13{x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $52x$ | $+$ | $39$ |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  | $-$ | $40x$ | $-$ | $39$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{4}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4} & =q(x)p(x)+(-40x-39),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{2}-4x+13.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{4}=q(A)p(A)+(-40A-39I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & =q(A)p(A)+(-40A-39I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(-40A-39I) \\\\  & =-40A-39I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-37777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "15cb6b92d182e5278ecaaaa3821a9a850fd84daaabbb087c401a2c99368d7107"} {"question" "q-37777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-37777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-39A-40I"] ["math" "-40A+39I"] ["math" "-40A-39I"] ["math" "-4A-9I"] ["math" "-A-3I"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-37777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-40A-39I"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-85993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nand $f(x)={x}^{21}-16{x}^{19}+6x-3$, find $f(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-85993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda ) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{19}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-16\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{21}\\,-\\,16{x}^{19}\\,+\\,6x\\,\\,-\\,\\,3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{21}$ | $-$ | $16{x}^{19}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $6x$ | $-$ | $3$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(6x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{19}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(6A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(6A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(6A-3I) \\\\  & =6A-3I \\\\  & =6[\\begin{aligned}4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}24 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -24\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}21 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-85993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "15cb6b92d182e5278ecaaaa3821a9a850fd84daaabbb087c401a2c99368d7107"} {"question" "q-85993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-85993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -12\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 0 \\\\ 72 & -27\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 27 \\\\ 0 & -72\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}27 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -21\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-85993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-140602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{16}-4{x}^{14}+7x-3\\,$, find $f(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-140602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+12 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{14}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-4\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{16}\\,-\\,4{x}^{14}\\,+\\,7x\\,-\\,3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{16}$ | $-$ | $4{x}^{14}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $7x$ | $-$ | $3$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(7x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{14}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(7A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(7A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(7A-3I) \\\\  & =7A-3I \\\\  & =7[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -28\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -31\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-140602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "15cb6b92d182e5278ecaaaa3821a9a850fd84daaabbb087c401a2c99368d7107"} {"question" "q-140602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-140602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 14 \\\\ -42 & 30\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}25 & -42 \\\\ 14 & -31\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}25 & 12 \\\\ -24 & -31\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}25 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -31\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}31 & 14 \\\\ 42 & -25\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "q-140602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}25 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -31\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "96a13739d7a610dc9cb395580389e4bd325188e2a3db4bd945101b73359b7104"} {"question" "e-5894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/assets/cb48b48ef82e5d6cfe903488e80a8cbd48e286c864ba387450e01430e718d77c.png) find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/example-5894.json" "file_sha256" "d7f90c3ad3c27488da5873f508cb16d0ff771495af6ce17339eaeb9281acd9f0"} {"question" "e-5894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda),$ i.e., the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2 = \\textrm{tr}(A)= 9+(-6)=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/example-5894.json" "file_sha256" "d7f90c3ad3c27488da5873f508cb16d0ff771495af6ce17339eaeb9281acd9f0"} {"question" "e-5511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\textrm{tr}(A)=-4$ and $\\textrm{det}(A)=-18.$ If $A^2 = xA + yI$ for constants $x$ and $y,$ then find the value of $x+y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/example-5511.json" "file_sha256" "bcb30d0842bf6dad542b6e92bfab23d81a797bcb62bd66e8bb61a4d77471566d"} {"question" "e-5511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda)=\\lambda^2-\\textrm{tr}(A)\\lambda+\\textrm{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =-4A+18I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x+y = -4+18 = 14.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/example-5511.json" "file_sha256" "bcb30d0842bf6dad542b6e92bfab23d81a797bcb62bd66e8bb61a4d77471566d"} {"question" "e-5510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/assets/b2240a11f1760050914a9c02554e0b7e86cd84d420fb437a43d95b474103b26c.png) and $A^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/example-5510.json" "file_sha256" "85b381ca8578253e424c03b568f177c63b6aff52d8cc219ed95d3a26ff8e11b4"} {"question" "e-5510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-8-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )+54 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}+A-2I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}+A-2I) & =O \\\\ A+I-2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{1}{2}I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ $q=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/example-5510.json" "file_sha256" "85b381ca8578253e424c03b568f177c63b6aff52d8cc219ed95d3a26ff8e11b4"} {"question" "e-5512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/assets/308850190e8483ae48e8179585c01f1805e6caf92e4d4254c996bd0ffa800427.png) and $f(x) = x^{12} -x^{10} +2x-1,$ find $f(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/example-5512.json" "file_sha256" "d035f562be78a1af73bcfd412f1e2eb23987f3f076f7ca9d5191f9b49c508a25"} {"question" "e-5512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+15 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\:\\:x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x^2-1\\:$ | $\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{x^{12} \\, - \\, x^{10}  +  2x  -  1 {\\phantom{|}} }$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | $x^{12}$ | $-$ | $x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $2x$ | $-$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{10}({x}^{2}-1)+(2x-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x) = x^{10}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = q(A)p(A)+(2A-I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(2A-I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(2A-I) \\\\  & =2A-I \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/example-5512.json" "file_sha256" "d035f562be78a1af73bcfd412f1e2eb23987f3f076f7ca9d5191f9b49c508a25"}] "usage" {"e-5894" [] "q-37779" [] "e-5511" [] "e-5512" [] "e-5510" []} "basis" 1866 "retractions" [[17592186125013 :question/problem "Given that $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$ and $A^{-1} = pI + qA$, find the value of $p + q$."] [17592186124279 :question/problem "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$ find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$."] [17592186124279 :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(λ) &= λ^{2} - tr(A)λ + det(A)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda)$, i.e., the eigenvalues of $A$, satisfy\n\n$$\n{λ_{1} + λ_{2} = tr(A); λ_{1} \\cdot λ_{2} = det(A)\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2 = \\textrm{tr}(A)= 9+(-6)=3\n$$"] [17592186124264 :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\textrm{tr}(A)=-4$ and $\\textrm{det}(A)=-18$. If $A^2 = xA + yI$ for constants $x$ and $y$, then find the value of $x+y$."] [17592186124264 :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\np(\\lambda)=\\lambda^2-\\textrm{tr}(A)\\lambda+\\textrm{det}(A)\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(A) &= O \\\\\nA^{2} - tr(A)A + det(A)I &= O \\\\\nA^{2} &= tr(A)A - det(A)I \\\\\nA^{2} &= -4A + 18I\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx+y = -4+18 = 14\n$$"] [17592186124263 :question/problem "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$ and $A^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q$."] [17592186124263 :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}-8 - λ & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(-8 - λ)(7 - λ) + 54 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + λ - 2 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{2} + A - 2I &= O\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by $A^{-1}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1}(A^{2} + A - 2I) &= O \\\\\nA + I - 2A^{-1} &= O \\\\\nA^{-1} &= \\frac{1}{2}A + \\frac{1}{2}I\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\nq=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\np+q=\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}=1\n$$"] [17592186124265 :question/problem "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $f(x) = x^{12} -x^{10} +2x-1$, find $f(A)$."] [17592186124265 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(λ) &= det(A - λI) \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}4 - λ & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4 - λ\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (4 - λ)(-4 - λ) + 15 \\\\\n&= λ^{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\:\\:x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x^2-1\\:$ | $\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{x^{12} \\,-\\, x^{10} + 2x - 1 {\\phantom{\\mid}}}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | $x^{12}$ | $-$ | $x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $2x$ | $-$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= q(x)p(x) + r(x) \\\\\n&= x^{10}(x^{2} - 1) + (2x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nq(x) = x^{10}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $A$, we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = q(A)p(A)+(2A-I)\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(A) &= q(A)p(A) + (2A - I) \\\\\n&= q(A) \\cdot O + (2A - I) \\\\\n&= 2A - I \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -8\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186123101 17592186123102 17592186123103 17592186123104 17592186123105] "immutable_fields" [[17592186123100 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123100 :answer-field/id] [17592186123100 :answer-field/key] [17592186123100 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
